This is the FINAL FRONTEND QUALITY PASS for EVYS Medical.

The major frontend functionality is now complete:

* Homepage
* Catalogue
* Categories
* Search
* Mega Menu
* Product Detail
* Quick Order
* Basket
* Responsive layouts
* Loading states
* Empty states
* Error states
* Interaction states
* Accessibility improvements

Do NOT add major new features.

Do NOT implement:

* backend
* database
* Stripe
* authentication
* real checkout
* real orders
* payment processing

The purpose of this pass is to evaluate the CURRENT WEBSITE AS A SINGLE PRODUCT.

==================================================

1. THINK LIKE A REAL CUSTOMER
   ==================================================

Navigate through the website as if you discovered EVYS Medical for the first time.

Test:

Homepage
→ Catalogue
→ Category
→ Product
→ Add to Basket
→ Basket

Also test:

Search
→ Product
→ Basket

And:

Quick Order
→ SKU
→ Product
→ Basket

Look for anything that makes the experience feel unfinished, confusing, cheap, inconsistent, or unnecessarily complicated.

Fix those issues.

Do NOT make changes simply because something could technically be different.

==================================================
2. ESTABLISHED BUSINESS TEST
============================

Ask throughout the audit:

"Does this look like a company that has been supplying medical and laboratory equipment for years?"

The answer should come from:

* hierarchy
* typography
* spacing
* product presentation
* information clarity
* restrained design
* consistency
* professional terminology
* interaction quality

NOT from fake claims.

Do NOT invent:

* years in business
* NHS relationships
* certifications
* customer numbers
* partnerships
* awards
* testimonials
* statistics
* reviews
* regulatory claims

Existing trust-bar claims remain mock/placeholder content and must not be expanded into additional invented claims.

==================================================
3. VISUAL CONSISTENCY AUDIT
===========================

Check every major page for consistency in:

* typography
* font weights
* heading hierarchy
* button styles
* border radius
* borders
* shadows
* spacing
* product cards
* badges
* icons
* form controls
* colours
* hover states
* focus states

The site should feel like one coherent design system.

Approved palette:

Ink:
#0B1726

Primary teal:
#087E8B

Accent teal:
#18A6B8

Background:
#F6F8FA

White:
#FFFFFF

Border:
#E5E9EE

Muted:
#667085

Success:
#16803C

Do not introduce random colours.

==================================================
4. REMOVE VISUAL NOISE
======================

Look for:

* unnecessary cards
* excessive borders
* excessive shadows
* repetitive sections
* unnecessary icons
* excessive rounded containers
* excessive badges
* oversized headings
* excessive animation
* redundant text
* sections that exist only to fill space

If something does not help the customer understand, browse, evaluate, or purchase a product, consider removing or simplifying it.

The goal is:

LESS, BUT BETTER.

==================================================
5. HOMEPAGE AUDIT
=================

The homepage is already visually approved.

Do not redesign it.

Check:

* hero hierarchy
* CTA clarity
* product/category discovery
* whitespace
* section transitions
* trust/value content
* product presentation
* footer
* mobile layout

Make only high-impact corrections.

==================================================
6. CATALOGUE AUDIT
==================

Check:

* product grid consistency
* filters
* sorting
* search context
* category context
* product card hierarchy
* pricing
* VAT presentation
* availability
* pagination/loading
* mobile filtering

The catalogue should feel efficient rather than decorative.

==================================================
7. PRODUCT DETAIL AUDIT
=======================

Check whether a customer can quickly answer:

What is this?

What does it cost?

Is it available?

What is the SKU?

What are the important specifications?

How many do I need?

How do I add it to my basket?

The specification section should remain particularly clear for laboratory and medical products.

Do not fabricate missing specifications.

==================================================
8. BASKET AUDIT
===============

Check:

* product identification
* quantities
* prices
* totals
* remove controls
* empty state
* CTA hierarchy
* mobile layout

The basket should feel like a serious purchasing interface.

==================================================
9. SEARCH + QUICK ORDER AUDIT
=============================

Check that there are two clearly different customer journeys:

BROWSE:
Catalogue → Product → Basket

BUY WHAT I ALREADY KNOW:
Search / SKU → Product → Basket

Neither should compete unnecessarily with the other.

==================================================
10. HEADER + FOOTER
===================

Check the header at:

* desktop
* tablet
* mobile

Check:

* logo hierarchy
* navigation
* mega menu
* search
* Quick Order
* account
* basket
* mobile drawer

Then audit the footer.

The footer should feel complete and professional without becoming enormous.

Use only information that actually exists in the project.

Do not invent company details.

==================================================
11. MOBILE QUALITY
==================

Test narrow mobile layouts.

Look specifically for:

* horizontal overflow
* awkward wrapping
* oversized whitespace
* tiny controls
* broken grids
* clipped text
* unusable specification tables
* sticky elements covering content
* navigation problems
* basket problems

Fix real issues.

Do not redesign mobile unnecessarily.

==================================================
12. ACCESSIBILITY
=================

Perform a final accessibility pass.

Check:

* semantic HTML
* heading hierarchy
* keyboard navigation
* visible focus
* button labels
* form labels
* alt text
* colour contrast
* touch targets
* modal/drawer behavior

Do not compromise the visual design.

==================================================
13. PERFORMANCE / FRONTEND QUALITY
==================================

Without introducing unnecessary dependencies, check for obvious frontend issues:

* unnecessary duplicated components
* excessive client-side work
* unnecessarily large assets
* broken image states
* console errors
* obvious React warnings
* inconsistent component implementations

Do not perform a large architectural rewrite.

Fix obvious issues only.

==================================================
14. PLACEHOLDER CONTENT AUDIT
=============================

Identify content that is clearly placeholder/mock data.

Do not remove useful mock content needed for the frontend demonstration.

But make sure placeholder claims are not presented as verified real-world facts.

Do not create fake:

* testimonials
* customer logos
* reviews
* certifications
* statistics
* company history
* regulatory relationships

==================================================
15. FINAL CLIENT-DEMO STANDARD
==============================

Imagine the client is sitting beside us and we open:

Homepage
Catalogue
Product
Basket
Search
Quick Order
Mobile view

There should be no obvious:

"this is just a demo"

feeling caused by poor UX, broken layouts, inconsistent styling, or unfinished states.

Mock data is acceptable.

Mock functionality is acceptable.

Unpolished UX is not.

==================================================
IMPORTANT
=========

